C11_NO_MEMORY_BASELINE [{"id": "C01", "raw": "The AX-731 nominal DC bus voltage is typically 750V DC. However, it's important to verify this specification in the specific documentation or manual for the AX-731 model you are working with, as variations can occur depending on the exact configuration and application.", "hit": false}, {"id": "C02", "raw": "I don't have specific information about the AX-731 continuous current rating. The AX-731 is a type of thyristor, but without consulting the datasheet or manufacturer specifications, I cannot provide an accurate value for its continuous current rating. To get the correct continuous current rating for the AX-731, you should refer to the official datasheet or contact the manufacturer directly. Datasheets typically provide detailed electrical characteristics, including continuous current ratings, for semiconductor devices.", "hit": false}, {"id": "C03", "raw": "To provide an accurate answer, I would need more specific information about the AX-731 device, as the overcurrent trip threshold can vary depending on the model and its configuration. However, generally speaking, the AX-731 is a type of circuit breaker or protective relay used in electrical systems to protect against overcurrent conditions.\n\nFor a typical AX-731 overcurrent relay, the trip threshold is usually set by the user or installer based on the system requirements. The threshold can be adjusted to a specific current level, often expressed in amperes (A), to ensure that the relay trips when the current exceeds a predetermined value.\n\nIf you have a specific AX-731 model and need to know the exact overcurrent trip threshold, you should refer to the device's technical manual or datasheet. These documents will provide detailed information on the adjustable settings, including the overcurrent trip threshold.\n\nIf you can provide more details about the", "hit": false}, {"id": "C04", "raw": "I don't have the exact rated output torque for the AX-731 actuator wit